
## What a Branch Is

- A branch is a separate line of development.

- The main branch is usually called:

    main

- I can create another branch to work on a feature.

- Example:

    main
      |
      └── feature-login

- I can work on `feature-login` without directly changing `main`.

- This is extremely useful for larger projects.

---

## Creating a Branch

- I can create a branch using:

    git branch feature-login

- I can switch to it using:

    git switch feature-login

- I can create and switch to a branch at the same time:

    git switch -c feature-login

- This is probably the command I will use most often.

---

## Viewing Branches

- I can see my local branches using:

    git branch

- The branch with `*` beside it is the branch I am currently using.

- Example:

    * feature-login
      main
      testing

---

##Switching Branches

- I can switch branches with:

    git switch main

- Or:

    git switch feature-login

- I should make sure I understand which branch I am on before making important changes.

- I can check with:

    git branch

  or:

    git status

---

## Merging Branches

- Once I have finished a feature, I can merge it into another branch.

- Example:

    git switch main

    git merge feature-login

- This takes the changes from `feature-login` and merges them into `main`.

- A common workflow is:

    Create feature branch
        ↓
    Work on feature
        ↓
    Commit changes
        ↓
    Push feature branch
        ↓
    Create Pull Request
        ↓
    Review
        ↓
    Merge into main

